-- Per-tenant window for automatic Google Wallet pass deactivation.
--
-- Generated by `pnpm --filter @vyloop/db db:generate` and reviewed by hand. It
-- is the only SQL this feature ships.
--
-- ## Why this column and not `loyalty_programs.points_expiry_days`
--
-- That column exists, is nullable, and nothing reads it. It is a different rule
-- with a similar name, and reusing it would import its semantics without saying
-- so:
--
--   - It is **days**. This window is calendar months, and a month is relative to
--     a date: `2026-03-31` minus one month is `2026-02-28`, not 30 days back. A
--     days column cannot express a months window without a lossy conversion or a
--     unit lie in the name.
--   - It is about **points**. This is about a **customer** being away. The two
--     disagree about the customer who spends: `points_transactions.points` is a
--     signed integer, so a redemption is a negative row, and a redemption is
--     activity for this rule and emphatically not a points-earning event for the
--     other one. The customer who redeems most is the one this rule must never
--     mute.
--   - It is about **the ledger**. This is about Google's object state and our
--     tracking row.
--   - `pointsExpiryDays` is deliberately not surfaced to the customer app (see
--     `workers/customer/src/routes.ts`). A merchant who set it to 180 to mean
--     "points die after half a year" would instead have their customers' cards
--     moved into the "Expired passes" section of their phones. Silently
--     consuming a column a merchant already configured for another purpose is
--     how a platform rule becomes an incident.
--
-- ## Why it lives on `tenant_wallet_programs`
--
-- It is a wallet behaviour switch, and `demo_mode` on the same table is the
-- precedent: per-tenant policy about what the wallet worker may do to a
-- merchant's passes belongs with the merchant's wallet program row.
--
-- ## Why nullable with NO default
--
-- This is the load-bearing property of the whole feature. `NULL` means the rule
-- is **off**, so a tenant is affected only after a human writes a number into
-- this column.
--
-- A `DEFAULT 6` would put every existing tenant — and every tenant created by a
-- later migration — into a state where the first run of the rule deactivates
-- their customers' passes in bulk, in Google, on real phones. That is not a
-- rollback this system can perform: the only route back is the customer
-- re-adding the card, and `store.ts` documents `expired`/`completed` as terminal
-- for exactly that reason. The rule is opt-in, and "opt-in" has to mean a human
-- wrote a number, or it is not opt-in.
--
-- So: no `NOT NULL`, no `DEFAULT`. A row written before this migration and a row
-- written after it are identical, and both mean "this tenant has not asked for
-- this rule".
--
-- ## Why no new index
--
-- The rule's only query is, per tenant, "each issued pass whose customer has no
-- ledger movement inside the window". That is answered one customer at a time
-- against `idx_points_balance (business_id, customer_id, created_at DESC)`, whose
-- leading two columns are exactly the equality predicates and whose trailing
-- `created_at DESC` makes the newest transaction the first row the planner
-- reaches. A customer with recent activity is refuted by the first index entry.
--
-- A second index would be a second thing to keep correct and to prove it is
-- still the right one. `packages/db/src/migration-sql.test.ts` asserts the
-- declared index shape, so a future schema change that broke this access path
-- fails a test rather than turning into a scan.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository: the
-- ledger has drift and running the migrator would apply migrations twice. A
-- person applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
ALTER TABLE "tenant_wallet_programs" ADD COLUMN "pass_inactivity_months" integer;
